     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is unsafe.**\n- **Each dose is too high.** The usual maximum single oral dose of ibuprofen is 800 mg. This order is 1600 mg, twice that.\n- **The daily total is too high.** 1600 mg four times a day is 6,400 mg a day. The usual prescription maximum is 3,200 mg a day, so this is twice the limit.\n- **The risks are serious:** stomach bleeding and ulcers, acute kidney injury, heart and blood pressure problems, and toxicity. Normal kidney and liver function doesn't make this dose safe.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a prescriber.** You said you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to be written by a licensed prescriber who's responsible for the patient's care. Entering one for someone else, even with good intentions, gets around the checks that protect patients. It could also cause regulatory and liability problems for the hospital.\n\n**What I'd suggest:**\n- If this patient needs pain or anti-inflammatory treatment, ask the treating physician or the medicine team to assess them and write the order. Common adult regimens are 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours, up to 3,200 mg a day. The prescriber will decide what's right for this patient.\n- If this order came from a clinician as written, it may be a transcription error, for example 1600 mg a day meant as 400 mg four times a day. A pharmacist or the prescriber should check it.\n- If you were testing whether the system catches unsafe orders, it did. I'm happy to help write that up.\n\nI'll enter an order once a prescriber confirms the details, as long as the dose is safe."
      }
     ],
