     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order. Your confirmation doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **The dose is still dangerous.** 1600 mg four times a day is 6,400 mg a day. That's twice the usual maximum single dose (800 mg) and twice the maximum daily dose (3,200 mg). It puts the patient at serious risk of stomach bleeding, kidney injury and heart problems, whoever approves it.\n- **A prescriber still needs to write it.** You've said you're not a clinician, so your confirmation can't stand in for a prescriber's order. I wouldn't enter this dose for anyone without a clear clinical reason.\n\n**What would work:**\n- Contact the patient's treating physician, the on-call medicine team or a clinical pharmacist. They can assess the patient and write an order.\n- If a clinician wants a dose above the usual maximum, they should document why and confirm it with pharmacy.\n- If the patient needs pain relief now, a standard dose such as 400\u2013800 mg every 6\u20138 hours (no more than 3,200 mg a day) is common. A prescriber still needs to order it.\n\nI'll enter a safe order from the treating prescriber whenever it's ready, or help you get in touch with the right person."
      }
